  :what-the-record-decides-it-by
  "TWO different predicates, in the same repository, and they are not the same
   predicate. (a) The CHECK's F3 leg, futon3 checks/find_snatch.clj:167-171:
   route is not :score-alone AND (string? warrant.file) -- two conjuncts.
   (b) The COLUMN's producer for find_organise's own table, futon3
   checks/find_organise.clj:567-571: the same two AND (vector? warrant.if-lines)
   AND (string? warrant.if-text) -- four conjuncts. (c) The producer of the LEAN
   literal's nonSelfCertifying column, futon2 u46_find_transcribe.bb:113-121,
   transcribes (a), the two-conjunct one, NOT (b). MEASURED ON THE PINNED FIXTURE
   (futon3 checks/find-snatch.edn, sha256 839897ef...): all 96 receipts carry
   :route :structured-antecedent, 0 carry :score-alone, and all 96 carry a
   warrant with :file, :if-lines, :if-text, :however-lines and :however-text -- so
   (a) and (b) agree on this record by both being true everywhere, and the record
   cannot tell which predicate F3 is."
  :expected-blindness
  "EXPECTED: `nonSelfCertifying` is blind to the WHICH in exactly the way slice 4
   found presence-F2 blind to the WHICH of the acknowledged clause. The warrant is
   the selected pattern's own file BY CONSTRUCTION in the producer (find_organise
   builds it as `warrant repository id` at :250 from `(get-in repository
   [:entries id])` at :216-220), so on the record every citation is self-owned;
   but nothing in (a), (b) or the Lean predicate COMPARES the citation's owner to
   the pattern the receipt is for. So a MISCITING finder -- receipts whose warrant
   is another repository pattern's text -- is expected to satisfy F1, F2 and F3 and
   to be indistinguishable from the faithful finder after erasure to today's
   Receipt. If it is distinguishable, the expectation is refuted and THAT is the
   finding. Written before the work so it cannot be back-fitted."
  :expected-two-versus-four
  "EXPECTED: the two-conjunct and four-conjunct readings of F3 are NOT equivalent
   as predicates, and the record cannot separate them because it satisfies both
   everywhere. So the difference between (a) and (b) is a live divergence in the
   code that no committed evidence bears on. If a receipt in the record fails (b)
   while passing (a), the expectation is refuted and the row is the measurement."
  :expected-non-buy
  "EXPECTED: F3 does not force a finder to cite anything real. A finder whose
   receipts set citesTextOrEdges := True and scoreAlone := False satisfies F3 for
   every selection, so F3 as carried costs a finder nothing. Asked as a
   measurement because it is what stops F3 from being read as the law that makes
   receipts mean something."}
